  "items": [
    {
      "id": "https://blog.google/quantum/willow-2",
      "url": "https://blog.google/quantum/willow-2",
      "title": "Willow 2: 105-qubit surface code operating below threshold \u2014 first logical qubit with net error reduction",
      "content_html": "<p>Google&#x27;s Willow 2 is a 105-qubit superconducting processor (transmon) that demonstrates the surface code operating below the error-correction threshold. The key milestone: a distance-5 surface code (25 data qubits + 24 ancilla qubits) achieves a logical error rate of 0.0003 per cycle \u2014 below the physical error rate of 0.001, proving that adding more qubits reduces the logical error (exponential suppression).</p><p>This is the first experimental demonstration that quantum error correction works as theory predicts \u2014 the &#x27;below threshold&#x27; regime. Distance-3 \u2192 distance-5 shows the expected exponential improvement. Extrapolation to distance-7 (49 data qubits) predicts 0.0001 logical error \u2014 sufficient for fault-tolerant quantum computing.</p><p>Willow 2 is available via Google Quantum Cloud. 67 research groups have access. The system runs the surface code at 1 MHz cycle rate (1\u00b5s per syndrome-extraction round).</p>",
      "summary": "Google's Willow 2 is a 105-qubit superconducting processor (transmon) that demonstrates the surface code operating below the error-correction threshold. The key milestone: a distance-5 surface code (25 data qubits + 24 ancilla qubits) achieves a logical error rate of 0.0003 per cycle \u2014 below the physical error rate of 0.001, proving that adding more qubits reduces the logical error (exponential suppression).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-15T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Google Quantum AI"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - Quantum error correction",
        "Google Research blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Willow 2 surface-code benchmark (distance 3,5,7)",
        "format": "CSV + Qiskit shots",
        "size": "48 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/googlequantum/willow-2-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://deepmind.com/genie-3",
      "url": "https://deepmind.com/genie-3",
      "title": "Genie 3: 100B-param generative physics world model \u2014 interactive 3D simulations from text",
      "content_html": "<p>Genie 3 is DeepMind&#x27;s 100B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-2 routing) generative world model. The 2027 update adds full-physics simulation: fluid dynamics, rigid-body collision, electromagnetism, and thermodynamics \u2014 all generated from a text prompt and running at 60 FPS interactive on an H100.</p><p>On the PhysBench benchmark (50 physics scenarios \u00d7 5 modalities), Genie 3 achieves 0.92 correlation with traditional physics solvers (OpenFOAM, PyBullet, COMSOL) at 10000x speedup. Users can perturb the simulation in real-time (move objects, change gravity, add forces) and the world model adapts instantly.</p><p>Released as Genie 3 API (cloud) + Genie 3 Edge (12B variant, runs at 30 FPS on RTX 4090). 47 universities use it for physics education + research. The model generates worlds that are physically consistent \u2014 conservation laws, energy, momentum all hold within 2%.</p>",
      "summary": "Genie 3 is DeepMind's 100B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-2 routing) generative world model. The 2027 update adds full-physics simulation: fluid dynamics, rigid-body collision, electromagnetism, and thermodynamics \u2014 all generated from a text prompt and running at 60 FPS interactive on an H100.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-17T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "DeepMind Genie team"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Generative physics worlds",
        "DeepMind blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "PhysBench benchmark (50 scenarios \u00d7 5 physics modalities)",
        "format": "MP4 + JSON",
        "size": "2.8 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/deepmind/genie-3-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-opus",
      "url": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-opus",
      "title": "Claude 5 Opus: 1T-param reasoning model with 1M-token thinking \u2014 solves 3 IMO gold problems",
      "content_html": "<p>Claude 5 Opus is Anthropic&#x27;s 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 512 experts, top-8 routing) reasoning model. The &#x27;Opus&#x27; variant extends Claude 5&#x27;s thinking budget from 100k to 1 million tokens, enabling multi-hour deductive reasoning. Active params: ~80B per token (top-8 of 1T).</p><p>On the International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO) 2027 benchmark, Claude 5 Opus solves 3 of 6 problems at gold-medal level (geometry, number theory, combinatorics). This is the first AI to solve IMO problems without hints \u2014 the previous best (o3-pro science) solved 1 of 6.</p><p>On ARC-AGI-2, Claude 5 Opus scores 0.83 (beating Claude 5 0.71, o3-pro 0.62). On the METR agent benchmark (long-horizon tasks), it completes 47% of 100-step tasks (vs Claude 5&#x27;s 22%). Released via Anthropic API; cost: $50 per 1M thinking tokens.</p>",
      "summary": "Claude 5 Opus is Anthropic's 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 512 experts, top-8 routing) reasoning model. The 'Opus' variant extends Claude 5's thinking budget from 100k to 1 million tokens, enabling multi-hour deductive reasoning. Active params: ~80B per token (top-8 of 1T).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-18T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Anthropic Research"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Constitutional reasoning",
        "Anthropic blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "IMO 2027 benchmark (6 problems \u00d7 5 attempts)",
        "format": "LaTeX + JSON",
        "size": "24 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/anthropic/claude-5-opus-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-6",
      "url": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-6",
      "title": "ESM-6: 500B-param generative protein model \u2014 68% first-try foldable + enzyme design",
      "content_html": "<p>ESM-6 is Meta&#x27;s 500B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-4 routing) generative protein design + function model. The v6 release adds an enzyme-design head that produces novel enzymes with specified catalytic activity \u2014 23 of 50 designed enzymes show measurable catalytic activity in vitro (vs ESM-5&#x27;s 12/50).</p><p>On the ProteinGym benchmark (47 design tasks), ESM-6 achieves 0.88 designability (vs ESM-5 0.82, RFdiffusion 0.68). First-try foldable rate: 68% (vs ESM-5 52%, ESM-4 38%, ESM-3 18% \u2014 the curve is approaching saturation).</p><p>Key innovation: a multi-modal protein tokenizer that encodes sequence, structure, and function in a unified latent space. This allows cross-modal generation: given a function, generate a sequence; given a structure, generate a function; given a sequence, predict structure+function.</p>",
      "summary": "ESM-6 is Meta's 500B-parameter (MoE 128 experts, top-4 routing) generative protein design + function model. The v6 release adds an enzyme-design head that produces novel enzymes with specified catalytic activity \u2014 23 of 50 designed enzymes show measurable catalytic activity in vitro (vs ESM-5's 12/50).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-16T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Meta AI FAIR + EvolutionaryScale"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Biology - Generative protein FM",
        "Meta AI blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "ProteinGym v3 + EnzymeBench (50 enzyme designs)",
        "format": "FASTA + PDB + CSV",
        "size": "3.2 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/evolutionaryscale/esm-6-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://quantinuum.com/helios-2027",
      "url": "https://quantinuum.com/helios-2027",
      "title": "Quantinuum Helios: 10,000-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer with fault-tolerant operation",
      "content_html": "<p>Quantinuum Helios is a 10,000-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer (Ytterbium-171 + Barium-138 dual-species). The dual-species architecture enables native mid-circuit measurement + feedforward \u2014 the first hardware to support real-time quantum error correction without slow optical readout.</p><p>Helios runs a distance-7 surface code (49 data + 48 ancilla = 97 qubits per logical qubit) at 0.0001 logical error rate \u2014 fault-tolerant threshold. The remaining 9,903 qubits support 102 logical qubits \u2014 sufficient for Shor&#x27;s algorithm on RSA-2048 (estimated 20M logical-qubit-cycles, ~4 hours on Helios).</p><p>Available via Quantinuum Cloud. 120 university + 47 industry research groups. The system&#x27;s fully-connected topology + 0.005% two-qubit gate error + mid-circuit measurement make it the leading platform for fault-tolerant quantum computing.</p>",
      "summary": "Quantinuum Helios is a 10,000-qubit trapped-ion quantum computer (Ytterbium-171 + Barium-138 dual-species). The dual-species architecture enables native mid-circuit measurement + feedforward \u2014 the first hardware to support real-time quantum error correction without slow optical readout.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-14T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Quantinuum"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - Large-scale trapped-ion QC",
        "Quantinuum blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Helios QEC benchmark (distance 3,5,7,9 + Shor estimate)",
        "format": "CSV + Qiskit shots",
        "size": "180 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/quantinuum/helios-2027"
      }
    },
